.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-UPDATE-APPLICATION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.33" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-update-application \- change an application's definition
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap update-application
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-runtime\fR|\fB\-r\fR \fIRUNTIME\fR]
[\fB\-\-artifact\fR|\fB\-a\fR \fIKEY\fR]
[\fB\-\-command\fR|\fB\-c\fR \fICMD\fR]
[\fB\-\-arguments\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-environment\fR|\fB\-e\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-buckets\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-queues\fR \fILIST\fR]
[\fB\-\-min\-instances\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-max\-instances\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-ready\-timeout\fR \fIMS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Changes an existing application's definition. Only the options actually
given are altered, so one setting can be changed without resending the
whole definition;
.B \-\-arguments
and
.B \-\-environment
replace the current values rather than adding to them.
.PP
A running application is restarted to pick the change up, within a few
seconds: the artifact, the command, the environment and the credentials
a process was handed are all decided when it starts, so there is nothing
a running process could apply. Redeploying a new build of the same
artifact key is therefore an upload followed by this command.
.PP
The identity an application runs as cannot be changed here - delete it
and create it again instead. Neither can its bucket; the artifact key
must name an object in the bucket the application already has.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application to change. Required.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-runtime " " \fIRUNTIME\fR
What the artifact is started with: \fIJAVA\fR, \fIPYTHON\fR,
\fINODEJS\fR or \fIBINARY\fR.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-artifact " " \fIKEY\fR
Object key of the artifact within the application's bucket. Must exist.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command " " \fICMD\fR
Command to run instead of the runtime's default.
.TP
.B \-\-arguments " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated arguments passed after the artifact path; replaces the
current list.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-environment " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated \fIKEY=value\fR pairs; replaces the current set.
.TP
.B \-\-buckets " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated bucket names the application may use; replaces the
current list. The application's principal is re-granted, so the change
takes effect at once - running instances included, since this is
enforced where a request is served rather than where a process starts.
.TP
.B \-\-queues " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated queue names the application may use; replaces the
current list.
.TP
.B \-\-min\-instances " " \fIN\fR
Smallest number of instances the autoscaler keeps running.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-instances " " \fIN\fR
Largest number of instances the autoscaler may scale out to.
.TP
.B \-\-ready\-timeout " " \fIMS\fR
How long an instance may take to create its socket, in milliseconds.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The definition was changed. It is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the caller is not an authenticated
administrator, no application of that name exists, the runtime is not
one of the four, the artifact does not exist in the application's
bucket, or a bucket or queue named by \fB\-\-buckets\fR/\fB\-\-queues\fR
does not exist.
.SH EXAMPLES
Deploy a new build:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm upload-file --bucket apps --key inbox.jar --file target/inbox.jar
euclid-cli eap update-application --application-id inbox --artifact inbox.jar
.fi
.RE
.PP
Let it scale further:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap update-application --application-id inbox --max-instances 8
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-get-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1)
